This is a vulnerable-dependency finding. Lock and manifest files only list the package, so reading them tells you nothing new. To judge exposure use find_imports (is the package imported at all?) and, if you know the vulnerable function, find_callers. A package that is never imported may still be a transitive dependency of something else, so say that rather than dismissing it. Do not answer false_positive just because the package is not imported: it is installed, so use needs_review or likely_true_positive with lower confidence and explain. Reserve false_positive for when the installed version is outside the affected range. Prefer the upgrade to the fixed version as the fix.
{%- elif category == "secret" -%}
This is a possible hardcoded secret. Decide whether it is a real credential or a test/example/placeholder value, using the file path, variable name and comments.
{%- elif category == "config" -%}
This is a configuration finding. Decide whether the insecure setting applies in a deployed environment or only to local development/testing.
{%- elif weakness == "choice" -%}
This is a weak-algorithm or insecure-setting finding (for example DES, MD5, SHA-1, ECB, a predictable random generator, an unsafe cookie flag). It does NOT depend on untrusted input: the weakness is the choice itself, so do not dismiss it because no user data reaches the operation. Decide by purpose: is it used for something security-sensitive (passwords, tokens, session or authentication values, encrypting or signing sensitive data)? If yes it is real. If it only serves a non-security purpose (cache keys, ETags, test data, UI effects) it is a false positive.
{%- else -%}
This is a code finding. Decide whether untrusted input can reach the flagged operation without effective validation or encoding, and whether the operation is actually dangerous in this context. Do not assume what a helper method or sanitizer returns: look at its definition. A value that a helper replaces with a constant is no longer attacker-controlled.
{%- endif %}

